implementationNotes:
Exhaustive re-inventory (supersedes research-207.json's list, which predates #207 GREEN landing and missed two sites): exactly THREE display call sites print a clock-time string in the whole app on main:
1. AgendaList.svelte `timeFmt` -> `row-time` (2 usages: compact + day-group rows)
2. event/[id]/+page.svelte `timeFmt` via `timeRange()` -> `event-detail-time` (both editable-button and read-only branches; also composes a start–end RANGE)
3. +page.svelte `eventCreateStatusTimeFmt` via `eventCreateStatusFmt(at)` -> the event-creation success status announcement

All three currently build their string via `new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB'|'en-CA', {timeZone:'Europe/Tallinn', hour:'2-digit', minute:'2-digit', hour12:false or hourCycle:'h23'}).format(date)` — i.e. they already produce a 24h zero-padded 'HH:MM' STRING from a Date+timeZone, they just don't currently branch on the preference. This means the issue's `formatTime(hhmm: string, mode)` signature (string in, string out) is directly sufficient for all three — no separate Date-input formatter variant is needed. The recommended shape: keep each site's existing Intl formatter untouched (it stays the source of TRUTH for the Tallinn wall-clock HH:MM, tz-DST-correctness preserved verbatim per the "PRESERVED VERBATIM" / "do not diverge" comments guarding these blocks), and wrap its output: `formatTime(timeFmt.format(date), $timeFormatStore)`. This is a minimal, surgical change that never touches the TZ-sensitive formatter construction the code comments explicitly protect.

Proposed `formatTime` placement: `src/lib/preferences/timeFormat.ts`, next to `sanitize`/`readStoredTimeFormat`, following the file's existing doc-comment density:
```ts
export function formatTime(hhmm: string, mode: TimeFormat): string {
  if (mode === '24h') return hhmm;
  const [hStr, minute] = hhmm.split(':');
  const h = Number(hStr);
  const hour12 = h % 12 || 12;
  const meridiem = h < 12 ? 'AM' : 'PM';
  return `${hour12}:${minute} ${meridiem}`;
}
```
Output spec verified against the issue body's example and TimeSelect's own to12(): '19:00'->'7:00 PM', '00:00'->'12:00 AM' (h%12||12 = 12), '12:00'->'12:00 PM', '07:05'->'7:05 AM' (no leading zero on hour, minute keeps its zero-pad from the source string — never re-derive it). 24h branch returns the input completely unchanged — byte-identical to today's output, satisfying "In 24h mode... nothing changes from today."

Reactivity: NO new rune/store machinery needed. `timeFormatStore` is a plain Svelte `writable`; TimeSelect.svelte already proves the working pattern for Svelte 5 — `const mode = $derived($timeFormatStore)` — auto-subscription inside `$derived` (or read directly as `$timeFormatStore` in a template expression / a function called during render) re-runs on every store change. AgendaList.svelte and event/[id]/+page.svelte currently do NOT import timeFormatStore at all; GREEN adds the import and either a `const timeMode = $derived($timeFormatStore)` (mirroring TimeSelect) or an inline `$timeFormatStore` read at each of the ~4 call sites. +page.svelte's `eventCreateStatusFmt` is a plain function invoked once at creation-success time (not itself reactively re-rendered on a later preference change) — reading `$timeFormatStore` inside it at call time is correct and sufficient; it does not need `$derived` since it's an event-triggered one-shot format, not a live-updating display.

Range formatting OPEN QUESTION (event-detail-time's `timeRange`): today "19:00–20:30". In ampm mode, is the correct rendering "7:00–8:30 PM" (single trailing meridiem, common typographic convention when both times share AM or PM) or "7:00 PM–8:30 PM" (both explicit, needed when the range crosses noon/midnight, e.g. 11:30 AM–12:30 PM)? The issue body's own example only covers a single time ("7:00 PM"), not a range — this needs a decision before RED can write the event-detail-time ampm-mode assertion. Recommend: always show both meridiems explicitly (`${formatTime(start,mode)}–${formatTime(end,mode)}`) — simplest, always correct including cross-meridiem ranges, and TimeSelect sets no precedent against it since it never renders ranges.

AM/PM value strings: confirmed no Paraglide keys are needed. `messages/{en,et,lv,uk}.json` already show the house convention keeps "AM/PM" as a literal, untranslated label even in the profile_time_format_ampm/time_select_ampm_label keys across all four locales (Estonian did NOT adopt e.l./p.l.). TimeSelect's own `<option>` values are the literal uppercase strings 'AM'/'PM' hardcoded in the component, not routed through `m.xxx()`. formatTime should follow the identical convention: hardcode 'AM'/'PM' uppercase literals, no i18n indirection, no new message keys in any of the four locale files.

happy-dom testability: fully assertable with the established pattern (see page.profile-time-format.spec.ts and AgendaList.spec.ts/event/[id]/page.spec.ts): `@vitest-environment happy-dom`, `localStorage.setItem(TIME_FORMAT_KEY, 'ampm')` BEFORE `render()`, then `container.querySelector('[data-testid="row-time"|"event-detail-time"]').textContent`. formatTime itself is also a trivial pure-function unit-test target (no DOM needed) for the exact-output-spec cases (19:00, 00:00, 12:00, 07:05, minute zero-pad preserved). The "no stored-value change" Done-when item is verifiable via the existing submit-path specs (event create/edit) asserting the wire payload's HH:MM is untouched regardless of display mode — none of those specs read `timeFmt`/`formatTime` output, only the submitted value, so they should require zero changes.

Proposed lint-style "no hardcoded HH:MM rendering outside the formatter" spec, modeled on typography-scale.spec.ts's readdirSync+readFileSync pattern (no new dependency):

```ts
// src/lib/preferences/timeFormat.no-hardcoded-render.spec.ts
import { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join, resolve, relative } from 'node:path';
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';

const SRC_ROOT = resolve(__dirname, '..', '..'); // src/

// A 24h clock-rendering Intl.DateTimeFormat: hour:'2-digit' plus either
// hour12:false or hourCycle:'h23', within ~120 chars of each other.
const HARDCODED_TIME_RENDER =
  /hour:\s*['"]2-digit['"][\s\S]{0,150}?(hour12\s*:\s*false|hourCycle\s*:\s*['"]h23['"])/;

// Files legitimately allowed to format/hold 24h HH:MM without going through
// formatTime(): the formatter's own home, the input composite (rule 5's
// sanctioned exception, already reviewed), and data-layer builders that only
// ever construct wire payloads / generated dates, never display text.
const ALLOWLIST = new Set([
  'lib/preferences/timeFormat.ts',
  'components/TimeSelect.svelte',
  'lib/events/recurrence.ts',
  'lib/events/eventConvert.ts',
  'lib/entity/entityCreate.ts',
  'lib/seasons/seasonManage.ts',
  'lib/testing/timeControls.ts'
]);

function sourceFiles(): string[] {
  return readdirSync(SRC_ROOT, { recursive: true, withFileTypes: true })
    .filter((d) => d.isFile() && /\.(svelte|ts)$/.test(d.name) && !d.name.endsWith('.spec.ts'))
    .map((d) => relative(SRC_ROOT, join(d.parentPath, d.name)));
}

it('no source file outside the allowlist hardcodes a 24h time-rendering formatter', () => {
  const offenders = sourceFiles().filter(
    (f) => !ALLOWLIST.has(f) && HARDCODED_TIME_RENDER.test(readFileSync(join(SRC_ROOT, f), 'utf-8'))
  );
  expect(offenders).toEqual([]);
});
```
This asserts the DONE-WHEN directly: after GREEN, +page.svelte, event/[id]/+page.svelte and AgendaList.svelte must have deleted their private `hour12:false`/`hourCycle:'h23'` formatters in favor of calling `formatTime`, and the regex catches any future regression the same way.

New AM/PM-mode display specs (one per site) should follow the page.series-create.spec.ts try/finally pattern (dynamic-or-static import of `timeFormatStore`, `.set('ampm')`, assert, `finally { .set('24h') }`) or the TimeSelect.spec.ts suite-level beforeEach/afterEach reset — both already proven in the codebase, no new pattern needed. Expected new assertions per site: toast "2027-04-18 7:00 PM"; event-detail "7:00 PM–8:30 PM"; agenda row-time "12:00 PM" (noon — a good disambiguating fixture) or similar.
